5d71-8f63-a43865d31098" :question/math-academy-id "q-158052" :question/is-example false :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=-1,$ then $cos⁡(-x+24\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933487/assets/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $cos⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x+24\\pi ) & =cos⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 12) \\\\  & =cos⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x) & =cos⁡x \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $cos⁡(-x+24\\pi )=-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-158052/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94645507-186a-54a7-a96b-ccfb0fb1f769"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-158052"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-136545"] :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933487/assets/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nNotice that our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. If we reflect the graph over the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that $cos⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-a) & =cos⁡a \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
